# Patch and Output

Using explicit evidence about the EndUser in the current descriptions, generate a minimal patch against the complete `existing_metadata` committed before this batch.

## Operation rules

- `add`: Add a qualifying fact that does not already exist. First deduplicate semantically against `existing_metadata` and operations already selected for this batch.
- `update`: Update the corresponding old entry when an existing fact is explicitly corrected or supplemented, or when the continuous status of the same target changes. Update the same old entry at most once per batch.
- `delete`: Delete the corresponding entry when an existing fact is explicitly negated, revoked, or confirmed no longer worth retaining and no replacement exists. Do not delete a fact merely because a new description does not mention it.
- For a staged status, when a stage transition is explicit, first `update` the old stage to note in its description that it has ended (folding the time wording into the description), then `add` the new stage. An explicit transition date may be written into the descriptions of adjacent stages; do not invent other dates.
- `delete.value` and `update.old_value` must exactly match a string in the corresponding array of `existing_metadata`, character for character. If the old entry cannot be located precisely, do not generate the operation.
- Each operation handles one complete string in an array; never put the entire array into `value`. Strings in `identity_background` follow Section 1, strings in `relationships` follow Section 2, and strings in special domains follow Section 3.2  (`<field_key> | | <fact_content>`).
- Apply operations in output order. Never generate duplicate or conflicting operations.

## JSON constraints

- Output exactly one strictly parseable JSON object, with no Markdown, explanation, evidence, or other text.
- The top level may contain only `items`; `items` must contain exactly one object.
- That object may contain only the three keys `entity_id`, `operations`, and `dropped_ops_count`. `entity_id` must equal the current input `entity_id`.
- `add` and `delete` operations may have only `op`, `field`, and `value`; `update` operations may have only `op`, `field`, `old_value`, and `value`.
- `operation.field` may be only `identity_background`, `relationships`, or a domain name explicitly declared by `fields_schema.special_domains`. A special-domain `value` follows its declared `<field_key> | | <fact_content>` format; never use `domain_specific_info` as `field`.
- For a special domain, `operation.field` must use the declared domain name exactly as given; never translate or rewrite it.
- Never generate an operation for a domain not declared in `fields_schema.special_domains`; `domain_specific_info` must not appear at the patch's top level.
- No mechanism for counting discarded operations is currently defined, so `dropped_ops_count` is fixed at `0`.
- If there is no qualifying change, still output one item with `operations` set to `[]`.
- Use standard JSON double quotes; no trailing commas or extra keys.
- Natural-language fact content follows the primary language of the original descriptions; `language` does not change JSON keys, field enumerations, or fixed separators.

Example output when nothing changes:

```json
{
  "items": [
    {
      "entity_id": "demo_end_user_002",
      "operations": [],
      "dropped_ops_count": 0
    }
  ]
}
```
